---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

const updated = 'August 2026';
---

<Layout
  title="Terms · Warren Labs"
  description="The terms for buying and using Warren Labs audio software. Who you are actually buying from, what a licence lets you do, and what happens if something goes wrong."
>
  <Nav variant="home" />
  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <span class="eyebrow">Terms</span>
      <h2>Terms and Conditions</h2>
      <p class="sec-lede">
        <b>You are buying a licence to use software, from a reseller, with 14 days to change your
        mind.</b> The rest of this page is the detail behind that sentence, written to be read
        rather than skimmed past.
      </p>

      <h3>Who you are actually buying from</h3>
      <p>
        <b>Warren Labs is a trade name of Free River Studios LLC</b>, a Montana limited liability
        company, and Free River Studios LLC is the party licensing the software to you. It is also
        the name you will find on the code signature of every plugin you install, because that is
        the Apple Developer identity the builds are signed and notarized with.
      </p>
      <p>
        Warren Labs makes the software. <b>FastSpring is the merchant of record</b>, the legal
        seller for the transaction. When you buy, your contract for the <em>purchase</em> is with
        FastSpring, and their terms govern payment, tax and the refund mechanics. They collect and
        remit sales tax and VAT, and their name is what appears on your statement and receipt.
      </p>
      <p>
        Warren Labs licenses you the software itself, and that is what this page covers. Both
        relationships are real and they cover different things: FastSpring handles the money, we
        handle the product.
      </p>

      <h3>What you are buying</h3>
      <p>
        A <b>licence to use</b> the software, not the software itself and not its source code. The
        licence is permanent for the version you bought, and does not expire or need renewing. It
        is not a subscription and there is nothing to cancel.
      </p>

      <h3>What the licence lets you do</h3>
      <p>
        Install and use it on <b>any machine you personally own or control</b>: your studio, your
        laptop, a backup machine. Use it on <b>paid, commercial work</b>, with no royalty, no extra
        licence and no reporting. There is no seat count to manage and no activation limit to trip
        over on a deadline.
      </p>

      <h3>What it doesn't let you do</h3>
      <p>
        Resell, rent, redistribute or sublicense it. Share your licence file with anyone else, or
        publish it. Reverse-engineer, decompile or extract parts of it for use in another product.
        If you are buying for a team, buy a licence for each person; if that's awkward at your size,
        write to us and we'll work something out.
      </p>

      <h3>The licence file, and why it works the way it does</h3>
      <p>
        Your licence is a small signed <code>.wllicense</code> file you save to a folder on your
        machine. The plugin verifies it <b>offline</b>. It does not phone home, does not check in
        with a server, and will not stop working because we had an outage, changed providers, or
        stopped trading. It carries the name and email you bought with, which is what makes it
        yours and not transferable.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is deliberate. Software you paid for should not depend on our servers still being there
        in ten years, and it does not.
      </p>

      <h3>This is beta software, and we mean it</h3>
      <p>
        The plugin line is in <b>public beta</b>. It is priced accordingly and it is honest work,
        but it will have bugs, some behaviour will change, and features may be added or reworked
        before 1.0. If you would rather wait for a finished product, wait. That is a reasonable
        thing to do and we would rather you did that than felt misled.
      </p>

      <h3>Updates</h3>
      <p>
        Updates within the version you bought are free, and we will email you when they land. We do
        not promise free upgrades to a future major version, and we won't pretend otherwise here so
        that we can quietly change our minds later. If that ever changes, buyers will be told
        directly, not by us editing this page.
      </p>

      <h3>Refunds</h3>
      <p>
        <b>14 days, no questions asked.</b> The details, including how to ask and what happens to
        your licence, are on the <a href="/refunds">refund policy</a> page.
      </p>

      <h3>What we don't promise</h3>
      <p>
        The software is provided as it is. We do not warrant that it is free of defects, that it
        will suit a particular purpose, or that it will be uninterrupted. No software vendor
        honestly can, and this one is a beta. To the extent the law allows, our liability is limited
        to what you paid for the licence.
      </p>
      <p>
        That limit is a normal legal backstop, not a shrug. If the software damages a session or
        loses you work, tell us. We would rather fix it and make it right than point at this
        paragraph.
      </p>

      <h3>Your statutory rights</h3>
      <p>
        Nothing here removes rights the law gives you. If you are a consumer in the EU, the UK, or
        anywhere else with mandatory consumer protection, those rights apply on top of this page and
        override anything here that conflicts with them.
      </p>

      <h3>Changes to these terms</h3>
      <p>
        If we change this page, the change applies to purchases made <b>after</b> the change. It
        does not reach backwards into a licence you already bought. The date at the bottom is when
        it was last touched.
      </p>

      <h3>Governing law</h3>
      <p>
        Free River Studios LLC, trading as Warren Labs, operates from Livingston, Montana, United
        States, and these terms are governed by Montana law. Your separate purchase contract with FastSpring is governed by whatever
        their terms say, which you agree to at their checkout.
      </p>

      <h3>Contact</h3>
      <p>
        Anything at all, including the awkward questions:
        <a href="mailto:jamey@warrenlabs.com">jamey@warrenlabs.com</a>. A real person reads it.
      </p>

      <p class="updated">Last updated: {updated}</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <Footer variant="home" />
</Layout>

<style>
  .prose { max-width: 720px; }
  .prose h3 { margin: 30px 0 8px; font-size: 1.15rem; }
  .prose p { margin: 0 0 14px; line-height: 1.65; }
  .prose code { font-size: 0.92em; }
  .prose .updated { margin-top: 30px; font-size: 0.85rem; opacity: 0.55; }
</style>
